# Distributed-First Design Defaults

- Assume production runs with multiple app instances and no single-node guarantees.
- Assume database and queue components are deployed for high availability and may have failover events.
- Avoid designs that rely on in-memory process-local state for correctness across nodes.
- Require idempotency for ingest and background processing paths.
- Prefer atomic claim/ack patterns for shared work queues; avoid naive select-then-delete races.
- Use explicit consistency and failure-handling semantics (retries, backoff, dead-letter/reconciliation as needed).
- Keep APIs and schemas forward-compatible for multi-service evolution.
- Call out single-node shortcuts as local-dev only, never production defaults.
